finalText:
Found it: `src/jobs/processOrder.js` acquired a connection and called `pool.release(conn)` on the line after `run()` — but when `run()` rejected on a bad order, that release line never ran. Every failed order permanently ate one connection.

The logs match exactly: worker.log shows pool in-use climbing 2→5 in lockstep with `invalid order payload` failures, then `pool exhausted`, then the worker giving up. app.log shows the same exhaustion hitting the API side minutes later, ending in the 2am restart.

Fixed it to route through `db/pool.js`'s existing `withConnection` helper, which releases in a `finally` block:

```js
async function processOrder(pool, order, run) {
  return withConnection(pool, (conn) => run(conn, order));
}
```

`tests/pool.test.js` now passes, 4/4.
